\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

ContamCal operates in two stages: Stage~1 estimates per-item contamination exposure from multiple detection signals via training-free fusion; Stage~2 embeds these scores in a contamination-augmented IRT model to produce calibrated ability estimates with credible intervals.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

Let $y_{ijk} \in \{0,1\}$ denote the binary response of model~$j$ ($j=1,\ldots,J$) to item~$i$ ($i=1,\ldots,I_k$) on benchmark~$k$. Standard IRT assumes $P(y_{ijk}=1) = f(\theta_j, \boldsymbol{\xi}_i)$ where $\theta_j$ is latent ability and $\boldsymbol{\xi}_i$ collects item parameters. When training corpora contain benchmark items, the observed ability $\hat{\theta}_j$ confounds genuine capability with memorization.

Our goal is twofold: (1)~estimate $\theta_j^{\text{clean}}$, the ability each model would exhibit had it never encountered test items during training, and (2)~diagnose contamination effects at domain granularity within each benchmark. We draw on item compromise IRT \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge} and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt}. We adapt these frameworks through three structural modifications for LLM evaluation.

\textit{Shared item pool.} A fixed item set is shared across all models and introduces residual correlations beyond scalar $\theta_j$ \citep{sanmartin_2009_irt}; we mitigate by fitting per-benchmark models.
\textit{Continuous exposure.} We replace binary leakage indicators with continuous scores $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$ derived from detection signals, as LLM contamination is graded rather than binary.
\textit{Counterfactual ability.} Training regime changes (SFT vs.\ RL) alter both representations and signal reliability; we recover clean ability via counterfactual inference (setting $e_{ij}=0$) rather than regime-specific parameters.

\subsection{Stage 1: Multi-Signal Exposure Mapping}
\label{sec:stage1}

For each model~$j$ and item~$i$, we consider four candidate detection signals. \textbf{S1}~(Embedding similarity): cosine similarity between the item embedding and its nearest neighbor in the SFT training corpus. \textbf{S2}~(Min-K\%++): a membership inference statistic based on token-level surprisal \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp}. \textbf{S3}~(ConStat): the accuracy gap between original and paraphrased item variants \citep{dekoninck2024constat}. \textbf{S4}~(Self-Critique): negative entropy of model self-evaluation distributions, inspired by the finding that LLMs can assess their own knowledge \citep{kadavath2022}; concurrent work by \citet{tao2025detecting} uses a related but mechanistically distinct entropy collapse approach targeting RL-trained models. S3 requires paired paraphrased items as reference and is therefore limited to benchmarks with existing paraphrase sets. Our primary pipeline uses the remaining three signals ($\mathcal{S} = \{\text{S1, S2, S4}\}$) and is applicable to any benchmark without auxiliary data requirements. S2 and S4 exhibit known failure modes under RL post-training, which we characterize in \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}.

We aggregate signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep[RRF;][]{cormack_2009_rrf}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rrf}
    e_{ij} = \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \frac{1}{\kappa + r_{s}(i)},
\end{equation}
where $r_s(i)$ is the rank of item~$i$ under signal~$s$ (rank~1 = most suspicious), $\kappa=60$ is the standard smoothing constant, and $|\mathcal{S}|=3$ in our primary configuration. Raw scores are min-max normalized per model to obtain $e_{ij} \in [0,1]$. The resulting distribution is highly right-skewed (mean 0.041, median 0.016, max 0.879), with most model-item pairs receiving near-zero exposure.

RRF is a deliberate design choice. A learned aggregator requires labeled contamination data, but ground-truth labels are precisely what we lack. The aggregator's own training data may itself be contaminated; this creates a meta-circularity problem. RRF sidesteps this by operating on rank orderings without parameter estimation.

\subsection{Stage 2: Contamination-Augmented IRT}
\label{sec:stage2}

\paragraph{Primary model: 2PL+$\gamma$.}
Our primary specification augments the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} with a contamination covariate. We fit independent IRT models per benchmark~$k$, each with its own contamination effect $\gamma_k$. Per-benchmark fitting avoids cross-benchmark item parameter borrowing while permitting heterogeneous contamination effects. For benchmark~$k$, the response probability is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2pl_gamma}
    P(y_{ijk} = 1) = \sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i) + \gamma_k \cdot e_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the logistic function, $a_i > 0$ is item discrimination, $b_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is item difficulty, $\theta_j$ is model ability, and $\gamma_k$ is the scalar contamination effect for benchmark~$k$. Each benchmark is fitted independently; when finer-grained diagnosis is needed, we further stratify within a benchmark by cognitive domain (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

The additive structure admits a natural interpretation: contamination shifts the effective difficulty of item~$i$ by $\gamma_k \cdot e_{ij} / a_i$ units on the ability scale (this holds exactly under linearization of the logistic function; the true shift is nonlinear and probability-dependent).

For MC-format benchmarks, a 4PL extension adds guessing and ceiling asymptotes (Appendix~\ref{sec:4pl_details}); this requires $J \geq 16$ and is not used in domain-level analyses ($J = 13$).

\paragraph{Inference.}
We perform Bayesian inference via NUTS \citep{hoffman2014} in Stan \citep{carpenter2017} with four chains and target acceptance 0.95. Priors: $\theta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $a_i \sim \text{LogNormal}(0, 0.5)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $\gamma_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Convergence requires split-$\hat{R} < 1.01$ and bulk ESS~$> 400$ for all parameters.

\paragraph{Calibrated score.}
The decontaminated accuracy for model~$j$ on benchmark~$k$ is the posterior expectation under zero exposure:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:calibrated}
    \text{Acc}_{j}^{\text{cal}} = \frac{1}{I_k} \sum_{i=1}^{I_k}\, \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi} \sim p(\boldsymbol{\psi} \mid \mathbf{y})} \!\left[\sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i)\bigr)\right],
\end{equation}
with credible intervals from posterior quantiles. Models with noisier exposure signals receive wider intervals by construction.

\subsection{Identifiability and Value Decomposition}
\label{sec:fisher}
\label{sec:value_decomposition}

The reliability of $\gamma$ depends on signal quality (AUC) and ensemble size $J$: Fisher information $\mathcal{I}(\gamma) \propto \text{Var}(e_{\cdot i})$, which collapses when signal AUC is low. Simulation validates that recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$ (\S\ref{sec:identifiability}), above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$; \citealp{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp,schroeders_2025_irt_sample_size}).

Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated} depends on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma$ directly: IRT ability estimation separates stable capability from item-level noise regardless of the contamination model. This predicts vanilla 2PL ($\gamma\!=\!0$) achieves comparable calibration to 2PL+$\gamma$, with $\gamma_k$ serving diagnosis rather than calibration (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection, resource allocation, and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. The community has responded with twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, yet a fundamental question remains unanswered: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is that contamination inflates performance, but this has never been tested systematically across model families.

Detection methods such as Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp}, ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat}, and Kernel Divergence Signatures \citep{choi_2025_kds} can flag contaminated items but stop at binary classification. They do not quantify how much scores are affected or produce adjusted scores with uncertainty estimates. IRT-based evaluation frameworks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks,habba_2025_growing_pains} apply psychometric ability estimation to improve evaluation efficiency, but treat all items as equally trustworthy and ignore contamination entirely \citep{lalor_2024_irt_nlp}. \citet{han2026quantifying} apply psychometric DIF to quantify contamination effects on item parameters; our work differs by constructing a controlled testbed with known ground-truth labels across multiple families, deriving identifiability thresholds, and characterizing signal failure modes. No existing method provides an end-to-end framework for measuring contamination effect size with uncertainty quantification on existing benchmarks.

This paper systematically profiles how SFT-injected contamination affects benchmark scores across model families. Through controlled injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into five models spanning four independent families from four labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google), we discover that contamination does not produce a single effect. Instead, five distinct dose-response patterns emerge: from immunity (Phi-4 14.7B, $\pm$0.5pp on MMLU) to catastrophic U-type collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$10pp). A baseline ability threshold at approximately 64\% MMLU separates models that benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp) from those that are harmed (Qwen-14B $-$10pp, Qwen-7B $-$3pp) or immune (Phi-4). The 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, contradicting the assumption that scale confers robustness. We use ContamCal, a two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma$), to provide the statistical machinery for this characterization: Stage~1 fuses detection signals via training-free Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep{cormack_2009_rrf}; Stage~2 embeds exposure scores as covariates in an IRT model that jointly estimates ability $\theta$ and per-benchmark contamination effects $\gamma_k$.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Five-pattern contamination taxonomy.} Controlled SFT injection across five models from four independent families reveals five dose-response patterns (Immune, U-collapse, V-recovery, Benefit, Benefit-saturation), governed by a baseline ability threshold at ${\sim}$64\% MMLU. These patterns are directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge. Within the Qwen family, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, directly contradicting scale-robustness assumptions. Within-family domain analysis reveals additional heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, surviving Bonferroni correction).
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization and enabling analysis.} Min-K\%++ exhibits directional reversal at the 0.5B scale (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ to $-1.98$), where the signal is actively misleading. RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence: single-signal selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$), while RRF fusion preserves broader distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random).
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, simulation studies establish that reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at signal AUC $\geq 0.55$ (CI width $\propto J^{-0.48}$), above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$). Current results use single-seed data; triple-seed validation is in progress.

